# Example reverse proxy for Student Panel using Caddy.
#
# Why: the app speaks plain HTTP. On its own, login credentials and the session
# cookie travel unencrypted — fine only on a trusted, isolated LAN. Put it
# behind HTTPS whenever it can be reached beyond that. Caddy is the least-effort
# option: it gets and renews certificates automatically.
#
# Install (Debian/Ubuntu): https://caddyserver.com/docs/install
#   1. Point a DNS name at this machine (e.g. panel.example.com), OR use a
#      local name + your own internal CA for a LAN-only box.
#   2. Put this file at /etc/caddy/Caddyfile (edit the domain + port).
#   3. In server/.env set  HOST=127.0.0.1  (only Caddy should reach Node),
#      TRUST_PROXY=1  (trust this one proxy's X-Forwarded-* headers), and
#      SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true  (you now always serve over HTTPS).
#   4. sudo systemctl reload caddy
#
# With TRUST_PROXY=1 the app reads the real client IP and protocol from the
# headers Caddy sets below (one hop).

panel.example.com {
	encode gzip

	# Forward everything to the Node process on localhost. Caddy adds
	# X-Forwarded-Proto/-For automatically, which is how the app knows the
	# original request was HTTPS.
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
}

# --- LAN-only alternative (no public DNS) ----------------------------------
# For an internal box reached by IP or a local hostname, you can let Caddy issue
# a self-signed cert with its internal CA. Browsers will warn until you trust
# that CA, but traffic is still encrypted. Replace the block above with:
#
# panel.lan {
# 	tls internal
# 	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:3000
# }
